\section{Introduction}
The California condor is a large scavenger native to the southwestern United States, 
identified by its 9 $\frac12$ foot wingspan and bald pink head. 
Scavenging birds such as these play a critical part in their native ecosystems, 
preventing the spread of disease by disposing of carrion before deadly bacteria 
are exposed to the environment.  Despite their presence in the United States for 
thousands of years, the introduction of the insecticide DDT in the 20$^{th}$ 
century caused their numbers to plummet to the tens by the 1980's. To prevent 
extinction, all 22 of the estimated birds remaining in the wild were captured and 
allowed to breed in captivity, where their numbers were gradually able to increase 
enough to begin reintroduction back into their native mountain ranges. 
\\
\\
In the wild, California condors breed slowly and deliberately. After reaching 
sexual maturity at around 8 years of age, a mating pair will produce a single egg 
that is incubated for around 6 months. After hatching, it will be dependent on its 
parents for another year. Even after leaving its parents, wild condors still face many  
from natural and man-made threats, leading to high rates of mortality. In captivity however, 
researchers have been able to increase the productivity rate of mating pairs through a process 
called double-clutching. During this process, the first egg produced by a mating pair is taken 
and cared for by researchers before being introduced to its natural habitat. Without viable offspring,
the mating pair will often try again, doubling the number of young produced in a year. 
\\
\\
In this paper, we create a model of changing condor populations in the wild and in captivity 
with and without double clutching. Our model takes into account condor age, productivity, 
mortality, and the differences in these between captive and wild birds. In developing this model, 
we hope to better understand the factors that impact changes to condor populations, and the impacts 
of repopulation efforts on condor numbers in captivity and in the wild. 

\section{Methods}
The goal of our model is to represent changing condor populations in captivity and in the wild under
varying assumptions. The model was built using MATLAB and follows a constrained population growth model. 
We assume initial populations of (NUMBER OF BIRDS). We assume equal male and female populations, that all 
birds mate and attempt reproduction, and sexual maturity at 8 years for wild condors and 7 years in 
captivity. We also assume fecundity rates of 1.8 and 0.9 in captivity with and without double clutching 
respectively, and 0.3729 in the wild. To stimulate wild populations, 80$\%$ of captive condors aged 1 year are 
released every year, however if the number of captive condors exceeds the carrying capacity of 150, all 
juvenile birds are released into the wild. The carrying capacity for wild condors is set to be 400 birds. 
\\
\\
We assume the following connected models for population growth between captive and wild condors. We 
assign to stock variables captive and wild population numbers at every relevant year of age. The flow 
variable between years for each model is growth, impacted by the ordinary variable of mortality rate. The 
flow variables taking captive to wild birds for years 1 through 6 represent transfer of captive birds to the 
wild. This transfer is informed by the ordinary variables of captive carrying capacity and rate of transfer.  


